"An exponent one-fifth algorithm for deterministic integer factorisation (https://arxiv.org/html/2010.05450)\nciteturn0view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2010.05450\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 569\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract. L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Preliminaries L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Integer arithmetic L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Polynomial arithmetic L20:     3. cite10†2.3 Strassen’s method L21:     4. cite11†2.4 Finding elements of large order L22:   4. cite12†3 Lehman’s strategy L23:   5. cite13†4 The main algorithm L24:   6. cite14†References L25: \nL26: cite15†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: arXiv:2010.05450v1 [math.NT] 12 Oct 2020\nL29: # An exponent one-fifth algorithm for deterministic integer factorisation\nL30: \nL31: David Harvey Email address: d.harvey@unsw.edu.au Address: School of Mathematics and Statistics, University of New South Wales, Sydney NSW 2052, Australia\nL32: ###### Abstract.\nL33: \nL34: Hittmeir recently presented a deterministic algorithm that provably computes the prime factorisation of a positive integer $N$ in $N^{2/9+o(1)}$ bit operations. Prior to this breakthrough, the best known complexity bound for this problem was $N^{1/4+o(1)}$, a result going back to the 1970s. In this paper we push Hittmeir’s techniques further, obtaining a rigorous, deterministic factoring algorithm with complexity $N^{1/5+o(1)}$.\nL35: ## 1. Introduction\nL36: \nL37: Let $\\FF(N)$ denote the time required to compute the prime factorisation of an integer $N\\geqslant 2$. By “time” we mean “number of bit operations”, or more precisely, the number of steps performed by a deterministic Turing machine with a fixed, finite number of linear tapes [cite16†Pap94 ]. All integers are assumed to be encoded in the usual binary representation.\nL38: \nL39: In this paper we prove the following result:\nL40: ###### Theorem 1.1.\nL41: \nL42: There is an integer factorisation algorithm achieving\nL43: \nL44:  | $$\\FF(N)=O(N^{1/5}\\log^{16/5}N).$$  |\nL45: \nL46: One may show that the space complexity of this algorithm is $O(N^{1/5}\\log^{11/5}N)$, but we will not give the details of this analysis.\nL47: \nL48: We emphasise that the new algorithm is deterministic, the complexity bound is rigorously established, and the algorithm runs on a classical computer. If one is willing to relax these conditions, better complexity bounds are known:\nL49: \nL50:   * –\nL51: The class group relations method of Lenstra and Pomerance [cite17†LP92 ] provably runs in expected time\nL52: \nL53:  | $$\\exp\\big((1+o(1))(\\log N)^{1/2}(\\log\\log N)^{1/2}\\big).$$  |\nL54: \nL55: This is the best known rigorous bound for a probabilistic factoring algorithm.\nL56: \nL57:   * –\nL58: \nL59: The general number field sieve is conjectured to run in time\nL60: \nL61:  | $$\\exp\\big(((64/9)^{1/3}+o(1))(\\log N)^{1/3}(\\log\\log N)^{2/3}\\big).$$  |\nL62: See [cite18†CP05 , §6.2] for a discussion of the heuristics supporting this conjecture. This algorithm is responsible for all recent record-breaking factorisations, such as the factorisation of the 250-digit challenge number RSA-250 [cite19†BGG+20 ].\nL63: \nL64:   * –\nL65: \nL66: Shor’s factoring algorithm runs in polynomial time, specifically $(\\log N)^{2+o(1)}$, on a quantum computer [cite20†Sho94 ].\nL67: It is also worth mentioning the deterministic algorithm of Shanks [cite21†Sha71 ], which achieves $\\FF(N)=N^{1/5+o(1)}$, conditional on the (still unproved) Generalised Riemann Hypothesis. According to [cite18†CP05 , §5.6.4], the complexity of this algorithm is actually $N^{1/5}\\log^{4+o(1)}N$, which is slightly worse than our bound in Theorem cite22†1.1 .\nL68: The literature on integer factorisation is vast. For further pointers, see [cite18†CP05 ]. For the rest of the paper, we focus on algorithms that are deterministic, rigorous, and classical.\nL69: The naive trial division method, i.e., checking all possible divisors up to $N^{1/2}$, runs in time $N^{1/2+o(1)}$. The first algorithm faster than this was that of Lehman [cite23†Leh74 ], who obtained $\\FF(N)=N^{1/3+o(1)}$. Lehman’s main theorem shows that if $N$ is a semiprime, i.e., $N=pq$ for distinct primes $p$ and $q$, then there exists a pair of “small” integers $a$ and $b$ such that $aq+bp$ lies in a certain short interval (depending on $a$ and $b$).\nL70: His algorithm examines every integer in this interval, for every $a$ and $b$ in a suitable range, and succeeds in factoring $N$ as soon as it encounters $aq+bp$.\nL71: Shortly after Lehman’s work appeared, Pollard [cite24†Pol74 ] and Strassen [cite25†Str77 ] independently published two closely related algorithms achieving $\\FF(N)=N^{1/4+o(1)}$. Both of these algorithms depend heavily on the FFT (fast Fourier transform). Strassen’s method was revisited by Bostan, Gaudry and Schost [cite26†BGS07 ], and then again by Costa and the present author [cite27†CH14 ], who achieved\nL72: \nL73:  | $$\\FF(N)=O\\left(\\frac{N^{1/4}\\log^{2}N}{(\\log\\log N)^{1/2}}\\right).$$  |  | (1.1)\nL74: (The results in [cite26†BGS07 ] and [cite27†CH14 ] were actually stated in terms of the function $\\MM(n)$, the cost of multiplying $n$-bit integers. It is now known that $\\MM(n)=O(n\\log n)$ [cite28†HvdH19 ], and at the risk of anachronism, we will use this result in our historical discussion to simplify several complexity statements from the literature.)\nL75: Another family of algorithms achieving $\\FF(N)=N^{1/4+o(1)}$ is based on Coppersmith’s method [cite29†Cop97 ] for finding small roots of polynomial equations modulo $N$ (see also [cite30†Ber08 , §5]). These algorithms rely on fast lattice reduction techniques such as LLL. Their complexity is presumably of the form $O(N^{1/4}\\log^{k}N)$ for some small positive integer $k$, but the author is not aware of a precise statement in the literature.\nL76: This approach has the advantage that the space complexity is only $(\\log N)^{O(1)}$, comparing favourably to the $N^{1/4+o(1)}$ space complexity of the Pollard–Strassen method.\nL77: In 2016, Hittmeir established the bound\nL78: \nL79:  | $$\\FF(N)=O\\left(N^{1/4}\\exp\\left(\\frac{-C_{1}\\log N}{\\log(C_{2}\\log N)}\\right)\\log^{2}N\\right),$$  |  | (1.2)\nL80: for certain explicit constants $C_{1},C_{2}>0$ [cite31†Hit18 ]. This bound is still $N^{1/4+o(1)}$, but improves on the “optimised Strassen bound” (cite32†1.1 ) by a superpolynomial factor, i.e., by an arbitrarily large power of $\\log N$. Hittmeir’s algorithm relies on an trick that seems to have first been suggested by Pollard [cite33†Pol78 , §3]: if $N$ is a semiprime $pq$, then Euler’s theorem implies that\nL81: \nL82:  | $$\\alpha^{p+q}\\equiv\\alpha^{N+1}\\pmod{N}$$  |  | (1.3)\nL83: for any $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$. One may therefore try to compute $p+q$ (and hence factor $N$) by solving $\\alpha^{x}\\equiv\\alpha^{N+1}\\pmod{N}$, using for instance Shanks’ BSGS (baby-step/giant-step) algorithm for computing discrete logarithms [cite21†Sha71 ]. McKee and Pinch [cite34†MP96 , §2] turn this idea into a deterministic factoring algorithm with $\\FF(N)=N^{1/3+o(1)}$, but Hittmeir squeezes much more out of it.\nL84: Among other things, he shows that the relation $pq=N$ forces certain restrictions on the residue of $p+q$ modulo various small primes, which can be used to speed up the BSGS search.\nL85: More recently, in a spectacular preprint [cite35†Hit20 ], Hittmeir presented an algorithm achieving\nL86: \nL87:  | $$\\FF(N)=O(N^{2/9}\\log^{11/3}N).$$  |\nL88: Notice that $2/9=0.222\\ldots<0.25=1/4$. This result represents the first exponential speedup for the problem of integer factorisation since the 1970s. The algorithm has many features in common with [cite31†Hit18 ], but instead of searching for $p+q$, it searches for the linear combinations $aq+bp$ that arise in Lehman’s algorithm. The replacement for (cite36†1.3 ) in this context is the congruence\nL89: \nL90:  | $$\\alpha^{aq+bp}\\equiv\\alpha^{aN+b}\\pmod{p},$$  |\nL91: which follows from Fermat’s little theorem. His algorithm actually examines many more candidates than Lehman’s algorithm, but the complexity is drastically reduced thanks to the use of the BSGS search.\nL92: The algorithm described in the present paper achieves a further exponential speedup, from $N^{2/9+o(1)}$ to $N^{1/5+o(1)}=N^{0.2+o(1)}$. It is based largely on Hittmeir’s algorithm from [cite35†Hit20 ]. The main difference, and the source of the exponential speedup, is that we apply the BSGS search technique much more aggressively.\nL93: Whereas Hittmeir breaks up the search space into chunks and applies the BSGS search to each chunk separately, we show how to sweep through the entire space with a single BSGS search. For a more quantitative discussion of the relationship between the two algorithms, see Remark cite37†3.4 .\nL94: We also make one other small improvement to Hittmeir’s algorithm: we replace a certain multipoint evaluation step with an invocation of Bluestein’s algorithm [cite38†Blu70 ] (see Algorithm cite39†4.1 ). This saves a further factor of $(\\log N)^{O(1)}$ overall.\nL95: ## 2. Preliminaries\nL96: \nL97: In this section we introduce various notation and terminology, and recall several ingredients from the literature that will be needed later. All results stated in this section without specific references may be found in standard textbooks on computer algebra, such as [cite40†vzGG13 ] or [cite41†BZ11 ].\nL98: \nL99: For $n$ a positive integer, we define $\\lg n\\coloneqq\\lceil\\log n/\\log 2\\rceil$ if $n\\geqslant 2$, and $\\lg 1\\coloneqq 1$. Clearly $\\lg n=\\Theta(\\log n)$ as $n\\to\\infty$.\nL100: Given a list of $n\\geqslant 1$ elements of bit size $\\beta\\geqslant 1$, we may sort the list using the “merge sort” algorithm in time $O(n\\beta\\lg n)$ [cite42†Knu98 ]. Here we assume that the elements belong to some totally ordered set, and that we may compare a pair of elements in time $O(\\beta)$.\nL101: ### 2.1. Integer arithmetic\nL102: \nL103: Let $n\\geqslant 1$, and assume that we are given integers $x$ and $y$ such that $|x|,|y|\\leqslant 2^{n}$.\nL104: \nL105:   * •\nL106: \nL107: We may compute $x+y$ and $x-y$ in time $O(n)$.\nL108: \nL109:   * •\nL110: \nL111: We write $\\MM(n)$ for the cost of computing the product $xy$. As mentioned in the Introduction, we may take $\\MM(n)=O(n\\lg n)$ [cite28†HvdH19 ].\nL112: \nL113:   * •\nL114: If $y>0$, we may compute the quotients $\\lfloor x/y\\rfloor$ and $\\lceil x/y\\rceil$ in time $O(\\MM(n))$. We may therefore also compute the residue of $x$ modulo $y$ in the interval $[0,y)$ in time $O(\\MM(n))$.\nL115: \nL116:   * •\nL117: \nL118: More generally, for a fixed positive rational number $u/v$, and assuming that $x,y>0$, we may compute $\\lfloor(x/y)^{u/v}\\rfloor$ and $\\lceil(x/y)^{u/v}\\rceil$ in time $O(\\MM(n))$.\nL119: \nL120:   * •\nL121: Using the “half-GCD” algorithm, we may compute $g\\coloneqq\\gcd(x,y)$, and if desired find integers $u$ and $v$ such that $ux+vy=g$, in time $O(\\MM(n)\\lg n)=O(n\\lg^{2}n)$. In particular, if $\\gcd(x,y)=1$, we may compute the inverse of $x$ modulo $y$ in time $O(\\MM(n)\\lg n)$.\nL122: Let $N\\geqslant 2$. We write $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ for the ring $\\mathbb{Z}/N\\mathbb{Z}$ of integers modulo $N$. Elements of $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ will always be represented by their residues in the interval $[0,N)$. In particular, an element of $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ occupies at most $\\lg N$ bits of storage. We write $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$ for the group of units of $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$, i.e., the subset of those $x\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ for which $\\gcd(x,N)=1$.\nL123: \nL124:   * •\nL125: Given $x,y\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$, we may compute $x\\pm y\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ in time $O(\\lg N)$, and we may compute $xy\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ in time $O(\\MM(\\lg N))=O(\\lg N\\lg\\lg N)$.\nL126: \nL127:   * •\nL128: \nL129: Given $x\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ and an integer $m\\geqslant 0$, we may compute $x^{m}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ in time $O(\\MM(\\lg N)\\lg m)$, using the “repeated squaring” algorithm.\nL130: \nL131:   * •\nL132: Given $x\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$, we may test whether $x\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$ in time $O(\\MM(\\lg N)\\lg\\lg N)$, by computing $\\gcd(x,N)$. If this GCD is not $1$ or $N$, and if $N=pq$ is a semiprime, then we immediately recover $p$ and $q$ in time $O(\\MM(\\lg N))$.\nL133: ### 2.2. Polynomial arithmetic\nL134: \nL135: We always represent a polynomial in $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}[x]$ by a list of its coefficients. For $d\\geqslant 1$, let $\\MM_{N}(d)$ denote the cost of multiplying two polynomials in $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}[x]$ of degree at most $d$.\nL136: \nL137: ###### Lemma 2.1.\nL138: \nL139: If $d=O(N)$ then\nL140: \nL141:  | $$\\MM_{N}(d)=O(d\\lg^{2}N).$$  |  | (2.1)\nL142: ###### Proof.\nL143: \nL144: Using Kronecker substitution, i.e., packing the coefficients into large integers and then multiplying these integers (see [cite40†vzGG13 , Cor. 8.27]), we obtain\nL145: \nL146:  | $$\\MM_{N}(d)=O(\\MM(d(\\lg N+\\lg d))).$$  |\nL147: \nL148: Under the hypothesis $d=O(N)$ this simplifies to $O(d\\lg^{2}N)$. ∎\nL149: ###### Remark 2.2.\nL150: \nL151: In the above lemma, the hypothesis “$d=O(N)$” means that for every constant $C>0$, the lemma holds for all $d$ and $N$ in the region $d<CN$, where the implied big-$O$ constant in (cite43†2.1 ) may depend on $C$. For the rest of the paper, a similar remark applies whenever we use the big-$O$ notation in this way.\nL152: ###### Lemma 2.3.\nL153: \nL154: Let $n\\geqslant 1$ with $n=O(N)$. Given as input $v_{1},\\ldots,v_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$, we may compute the polynomial $f(x)=(x-v_{1})\\cdots(x-v_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}[x]$ in time\nL155: \nL156:  | $$O(n\\lg^{3}N).$$  |\nL157: ###### Proof.\nL158: \nL159: This is a standard application of a product tree: starting with the linear polynomials $x-v_{i}$, we repeatedly multiply pairs of polynomials together, with the degree doubling at each level. There are $O(\\lg n)=O(\\lg N)$ levels, and Lemma cite44†2.1 implies that the cost at each level is $O(n\\lg^{2}N)$. For further details, see for instance [cite40†vzGG13 , Algorithm 10.3]. ∎\nL160: ###### Lemma 2.4.\nL161: \nL162: Given as input an element $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{*}_{N}$, positive integers $m,n=O(N)$, and $f\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}[x]$ of degree $n$, we may compute $f(1),f(\\alpha),\\ldots,f(\\alpha^{m-1})\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ in time\nL163: \nL164:  | $$O((n+m)\\lg^{2}N).$$  |\nL165: ###### Proof.\nL166: \nL167: We use a variant of Bluestein’s trick [cite38†Blu70 ] (following [cite45†HvdH18 , Lem. 3]). Let $f(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^{n}f_{j}x^{j}$. Then the identity $ij=\\binom{i}{2}+\\binom{-j}{2}-\\binom{i-j}{2}$ implies that\nL168: \nL169:  | $$f(\\alpha^{i})=\\sum_{j=0}^{n}f_{j}\\alpha^{ij}=h_{i}\\sum_{j=0}^{n}f^{\\prime}_{j}g_{i-j},$$  |  | (2.2)\nL170: \nL171: where\nL172: \nL173:  | $$h_{i}\\coloneqq\\alpha^{\\binom{i}{2}},\\qquad f^{\\prime}_{j}\\coloneqq\\alpha^{\\binom{-j}{2}}f_{j},\\qquad g_{k}\\coloneqq\\alpha^{-\\binom{k}{2}}.$$  |\nL174: Using the identities $\\binom{i+1}{2}=\\binom{i}{2}+i$ and $\\binom{-j}{2}=\\binom{j+1}{2}$, we may compute $h_{0},\\ldots,h_{m-1}$, $f^{\\prime}_{0},\\ldots,f^{\\prime}_{n}$ and $g_{-n},\\ldots,g_{m-1}$ from $\\alpha$, $\\alpha^{-1}$ and the coefficients $f_{j}$ using $O(n+m)$ multiplications in $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$. For each $i\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m-1\\}$, the sum $\\sum_{j=0}^{n}f^{\\prime}_{j}g_{i-j}$ in (cite46†2.2 ) is equal to the coefficient of $x^{i}$ in the product of the Laurent polynomials\nL175:  | $$f^{\\prime}\\coloneqq\\sum_{j=0}^{n}f^{\\prime}_{j}x^{j}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad g\\coloneqq\\sum_{k=-n}^{m-1}g_{k}x^{k}.$$  |\nL176: \nL177: The problem thus reduces to multiplying a polynomial of degree $n$ by a polynomial of degree $n+m-1$, plus $O(n+m)$ auxiliary multiplications in $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$. The claimed complexity bound then follows from Lemma cite44†2.1 . ∎\nL178: ### 2.3. Strassen’s method\nL179: \nL180: The following result, which goes back to Strassen [cite25†Str77 ], may be used to quickly rule out “small” factors of a given integer $N$.\nL181: \nL182: ###### Proposition 2.5.\nL183: \nL184: Given as input a positive integer $M=O(N)$, we may test if $N$ has a prime divisor $p\\leqslant M$, and if so find the smallest such divisor, in time\nL185: \nL186:  | $$O(M^{1/2}\\lg^{3}N).$$  |\nL187: ###### Proof.\nL188: We give only a sketch; for details (and improvements) see [cite25†Str77 ], [cite26†BGS07 ] or [cite27†CH14 ]. Let $d\\coloneqq\\lceil M^{1/2}\\rceil$ and compute $f(x)=(x+1)\\cdots(x+d)\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}[x]$ using Lemma cite47†2.3 . Then evaluate $f(x)$ simultaneously at $x=0,d,\\ldots,(d-1)d$, using a fast multipoint evaluation algorithm.\nL189: If any $f(jd)=(jd+1)\\cdots(jd+d)$ has non-trivial GCD with $N$, then one of its constituents $jd+i$ must have non-trivial GCD with $N$, and this observation may be used to locate any prime divisors of $N$ such that $p\\leqslant d^{2}$ (using a similar procedure to the closely related Algorithm cite39†4.1 of the present paper). ∎\nL190: ###### Remark 2.6.\nL191: \nL192: Taking $M\\coloneqq\\lfloor N^{1/2}\\rfloor$ in the above result leads directly to Strassen’s $N^{1/4+o(1)}$ integer factorisation algorithm.\nL193: ### 2.4. Finding elements of large order\nL194: \nL195: The following result, which allows us to find elements of large order in $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$, is due to Hittmeir. Of course in practice this is easy, because almost every element of $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$ has large order. The point of Hittmeir’s result is that the algorithm is deterministic and gives provably correct output.\nL196: ###### Proposition 2.7 ([cite31†Hit18 , Theorem 6.3]).\nL197: \nL198: There is an algorithm with the following properties. It takes as input integers $N\\geqslant 2$ and $D$ such that $N^{2/5}\\leqslant D\\leqslant N$. It returns either some $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$ such that $\\ord_{N}(\\alpha)>D$, or a nontrivial factor of $N$, or “$N$ is prime”. Its running time is\nL199: \nL200:  | $$O(D^{1/2}\\lg^{2}N).$$  |\nL201: The algorithm is somewhat involved, and we give only a brief summary here. The idea is to try to compute the orders in $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ of candidates $\\alpha=2,3$, and so on, using a BSGS search up to some bound. If for some candidate $\\alpha$ we fail to compute the order, then the order must be large and we are done. On the other hand, if we succeed in computing the order of a candidate, then this reveals valuable information about the possible prime divisors of $N$.\nL202: Continuing in this way, Hittmeir shows that eventually we recover enough information about these divisors that it becomes feasible to search for them directly using a variant of the algorithm of Proposition cite48†2.5 . For details, see [cite31†Hit18 ].\nL203: ###### Remark 2.8.\nL204: \nL205: According to [cite31†Hit18 , Remark 6.4], the assumption ${D\\geqslant N^{2/5}}$ is not the sharpest possible; the same result may be proved under the weaker hypothesis $D=\\Omega(N^{1/3+o(1)})$ for suitable $o(1)$. Luckily for us, the $N^{2/5}$ bound as stated is good enough for our application (but only just).\nL206: ## 3. Lehman’s strategy\nL207: \nL208: Given an integer $N$ that is either prime or a semiprime $pq$, the main algorithm in Section cite13†4 will attempt to factor $N$ by searching for integers of the form $aq+bp$ for various small values of $a$ and $b$. Before considering Lehman’s theorem in detail, we show (following [cite35†Hit20 , Lem. 4.1]) that it is easy to recover $p$ and $q$ once we have guessed the correct value of $aq+bp$.\nL209: ###### Lemma 3.1.\nL210: \nL211: Given as input an integer $N\\geqslant 2$ such that $N$ is either a prime or a semiprime $pq$, and positive integers $a,b,u=O(N)$, we may test if $u$ is of the form $aq+bp$, and if so recover $p$ and $q$, in time $O(\\MM(\\lg N))$.\nL212: ###### Proof.\nL213: \nL214: If $N=pq$ and $u=aq+bp$, then $aq$ and $bp$ are roots of the polynomial $Q(y)\\coloneqq y^{2}-uy+abN$. We first check if $Q(y)$ has rational roots, by testing whether $u^{2}-4abN$ is a square, and if so, compute the roots via the quadratic formula. It is then straightforward to recover $p$ and $q$, if they exist. ∎\nL215: ###### Remark 3.2.\nL216: \nL217: In fact, as is implicit in Lehman’s paper, we may still recover $p$ and $q$ without knowing $a$ and $b$, provided that we know the product $ab$ and that $\\gcd(ab,N)=1$. This requires one additional GCD operation, so the cost rises to $O(\\MM(\\lg N)\\lg\\lg N)$.\nL218: Now we state a result that is almost equivalent to Lehman’s main theorem in [cite23†Leh74 ]. Actually, Lehman’s original statement would be perfectly adequate for our purposes, but we have chosen to present a slightly different version, together with a considerably simpler proof (inspired by the presentation of Lehman’s algorithm in [cite18†CP05 , Thm. 5.1.3]).\nL219: ###### Lemma 3.3.\nL220: \nL221: Let $p,q$ and $r$ be positive integers, and let $N=pq$. Assume that\nL222: \nL223:  | $$(N/r)^{1/2}\\leqslant p<N^{1/2}.$$  |  | (3.1)\nL224: \nL225: Then there exist positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $1\\leqslant ab\\leqslant r$ such that\nL226: \nL227:  | $$0\\leqslant aq+bp-(4abN)^{1/2}<\\frac{N^{1/2}}{4r(ab)^{1/2}}.$$  |  | (3.2)\nL228: ###### Proof.\nL229: \nL230: Theorem 36 of [cite49†HW08 ] states that for any real number $\\xi\\in(0,1)$ and any integer $n\\geqslant 1$, there exist integers $a$ and $b$, with $1\\leqslant b\\leqslant n$ and $0\\leqslant a\\leqslant b$, such that\nL231: \nL232:  | $$\\left|\\frac{a}{b}-\\xi\\right|\\leqslant\\frac{1}{(n+1)b}.$$  |\nL233: \nL234: We apply this result for\nL235: \nL236:  | $$\\xi\\coloneqq p/q,\\qquad n\\coloneqq\\lfloor B\\rfloor,\\qquad B\\coloneqq(qr/p)^{1/2}>1.$$  |\nL237: \nL238: Since $n\\leqslant B<n+1$, we obtain\nL239:  | $$\\left|\\frac{a}{b}-\\frac{p}{q}\\right|<\\frac{1}{Bb}.$$  |\nL240: \nL241: The hypothesis $p\\geqslant(N/r)^{1/2}=(pq/r)^{1/2}$ yields $p/q\\geqslant(p/qr)^{1/2}=1/B$. Therefore\nL242: \nL243:  | $$\\frac{a}{b}>\\frac{p}{q}-\\frac{1}{Bb}\\geqslant\\frac{1}{B}-\\frac{1}{B}=0,$$  |\nL244: \nL245: showing that $a$ must be positive. Next, the estimate\nL246: \nL247:  | $$ab=\\frac{a}{b}b^{2}<\\left(\\frac{p}{q}+\\frac{1}{Bb}\\right)b^{2}=\\frac{p}{q}b^{2}+\\frac{b}{B}\\leqslant\\frac{p}{q}B^{2}+1=r+1$$  |\nL248: implies that $ab\\leqslant r$. Finally, the inequality of arithmetic and geometric means yields $aq+bp\\geqslant(4abN)^{1/2}$, so\nL249: \nL250:  | $$0\\leqslant aq+bp-(4abN)^{1/2}=\\frac{(aq+bp)^{2}-4abN}{aq+bp+(4abN)^{1/2}}\\leqslant\\frac{(aq-bp)^{2}}{4(abN)^{1/2}}.$$  |\nL251: \nL252: Since $|aq-bp|<q/B=(N/r)^{1/2}$, we obtain\nL253: \nL254:  | $$0\\leqslant aq+bp-(4abN)^{1/2}<\\frac{N/r}{4(abN)^{1/2}}=\\frac{N^{1/2}}{4r(ab)^{1/2}}.\\qed$$  |\nL255: ###### Remark 3.4.\nL256: \nL257: Lemma cite50†3.3 shows that the total number of candidates for $aq+bp$, i.e., the number of integers $x$ such that\nL258: \nL259:  | $$0\\leqslant x-(4kN)^{1/2}<\\frac{N^{1/2}}{4rk^{1/2}}$$  |\nL260: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv:quant-ph/9508027v2  25 Jan 1996 (https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027v2)\nciteturn0view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 28; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027v2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1862\n"
